<!--
  The whole cell: the arm with its suction tool, the pallet with its stack of
  30 modules, the conveyor with its torque tube, and a depth camera on a post.

    bazel run //:viewer_cell

  src/cell.h loads this file and owns what it builds. Every part keeps its
  own file and its own conventions (see the comment at the top of each); this
  file only puts them together and adds the camera.

  Order matters. arm.xml comes first among the parts, so the arm's six
  joints are the first entries of qpos and qvel and its six motors the first
  of ctrl. Its keyframe arm_home, and arm_clear below, give only those first
  six numbers; MuJoCo fills the rest from where everything was built, which
  puts the pallet, the stack and the tube at their nominal places. With any
  other part first those six numbers would land on a module's free joint.
  src/cell.cc does not rely on the order itself: it copies the keyframes'
  numbers through the arm's own indices.

  Layout, in the world frame of common.xml (origin on the floor under the
  arm's base, +X toward the pallet, conveyor on -X, +Z up):
    arm        base at the origin
    pallet     front module's glass centre at about (1.95, 0, 0.75), facing -X
               (pallet.xml sets it; nothing here or in src/cell.* repeats it)
    conveyor   tube along Y at x = -2.0, placement spot (-2.0, 0, 0.8175)
    camera     at (0, 0, 4.0), on a boom from a post standing at (0, -3.8)
    parking    modules carried away lie on the floor at x 0.85 to 13.55,
               y 11.45 to 19.55 (src/cell.h, ParkingPose)

  Names: every part prefixes its own (arm_, pallet, module_, conveyor_, tube,
  rail_, tool, suction); the camera's are camera_. Nothing clashes.
-->
<mujoco model="cell">
  <include file="common.xml"/>
  <include file="arm.xml"/>
  <include file="pallet.xml"/>
  <include file="conveyor.xml"/>
  <include file="suction.xml"/>

  <worldbody>
    <!--
      The depth camera and what holds it.

      Where: 4.0 m above the floor, straight above the arm's base, looking
      forward and down at the pallet. It has to see the whole glass face of
      the front module, which starts at about (1.95, 0, 0.75) with a full
      pallet and ends about 1 m further back, at (2.95, 0, 0.57), when only
      the last module is left, wherever the pallet was set down. If the
      pallet moves again, re-aim the camera and run tests/cell_test.cc,
      which checks this view against the pallet as pallet.xml builds it.

      Why so high: the arm stands between the camera and the pallet, and a
      camera lower down in front of the pallet would sit where the arm works.
      The tool tip cannot get higher than 3.53 m, even with the arm stretched
      straight up; the corners of the tool's plate, with the wrist bent, a
      little higher. At 4.0 m nothing the arm does reaches the camera, its
      housing or its boom: tests/cell_test.cc tries a few thousand poses
      across all the joint ranges and finds the arm never nearer than about
      0.25 m. The price is a slanting view: the camera sees module_0's glass
      about 49 degrees off square and module_29's about 39 degrees.

      The post stands on the floor at (0, -3.8), beyond the 3.22 m the tool
      tip can reach sideways, and on neither the pallet's nor the conveyor's
      side. A boom runs from its top along +Y to above the base, and the
      camera hangs under its end. All of it collides, so an arm path that
      hits it shows up as a contact rather than passing through.

      The arm at arm_home blocks the view: the forearm and the tool hang in
      front of the stack. At arm_clear (below) the arm faces the conveyor and
      nothing is in the way; tests/cell_test.cc proves it with rays.
    -->
    <body name="camera_post" pos="0 -3.8 0">
      <geom name="camera_post_column" type="box" size="0.075 0.075 2.125"
            pos="0 0 2.125" rgba="0.35 0.35 0.38 1"/>
      <geom name="camera_post_boom" type="box" size="0.05 1.975 0.05"
            pos="0 1.975 4.2" rgba="0.35 0.35 0.38 1"/>
      <geom name="camera_hanger" type="cylinder" size="0.015"
            fromto="-0.0405 3.8 4.1 -0.0405 3.8 4.15" rgba="0.35 0.35 0.38 1"/>
      <!--
        The housing sits wholly behind the lens: its front face is 0.02 m
        behind the camera's position, along the camera's +Z. So it is never
        in the picture, and a ray cast from the camera's position starts
        outside it.
      -->
      <geom name="camera_housing" type="box" size="0.06 0.04 0.05"
            pos="-0.040527 3.8 4.057075" xyaxes="0 -1 0 0.815360 0 0.578954"
            rgba="0.1 0.1 0.1 1"/>

      <!--
        The camera's frame, as for every MuJoCo camera: it looks along its
        own -Z, +Y is up in the image and +X is to the right. Here +X is
        world -Y (right, as seen from the arm looking at the pallet), and -Z
        points at (2.40, 0, 0.62), 54.6 degrees below the horizontal, the
        middle of the region the front glass can be in.

        fovy is the vertical field of view in degrees (MuJoCo reads it in
        degrees whatever the compiler's angle setting). With 640 x 480 square
        pixels the horizontal field is 51.8 degrees and the focal length
        240 / tan(20 deg) = 659.4 pixels. The principal point is the image
        centre. A pixel is about 5.7 mm across at module_0's glass and 6.9 mm
        at module_29's, seen square to the line of sight; the slant stretches
        that to about 9 mm along the glass's short side. The face's corners
        stay at least 80 pixels inside the image's edges (the left and right
        edges are the tight ones) for every pallet pose Reset can draw.

        The offscreen buffer is 640 x 480 by default, the same as this, so
        rendering at full resolution needs no <visual> setting. Rendering
        the depth image is the next stage; nothing here renders.
      -->
      <camera name="depth_camera" pos="0 3.8 4.0" xyaxes="0 -1 0 0.815360 0 0.578954"
              fovy="40" resolution="640 480"/>
    </body>

    <!--
      The check camera: on the floor, looking straight up at the module the
      arm holds over it at the check pose (joints 1-6 -85.00 -90.81 81.68 0
      99.13 90 degrees; src/held_module_finder.h, src/check_placement.h).
      fovy 61 degrees, 848 x 480, as on the rig (models/check_scene.xml). The
      housing is below the lens, wholly behind it. All of it collides.
    -->
    <body name="check_camera_stand" pos="0.1307 1.4943 0">
      <geom name="check_camera_base" type="box" size="0.12 0.12 0.01" pos="0 0 0.01" rgba="0.35 0.35 0.38 1"/>
      <geom name="check_camera_housing" type="box" size="0.06 0.045 0.06" pos="0 0 0.07" rgba="0.1 0.1 0.1 1"/>
      <camera name="check_camera" pos="0 0 0.15" xyaxes="1 0 0 0 -1 0" fovy="61" resolution="848 480"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <!--
    The arm's pose for taking a picture: arm_home turned half a turn about
    joint 1, so it faces the conveyor instead of the pallet, the tool hanging
    over the tube's side of the cell at (-1.55, 0, 1.395). The arm is then
    all on the conveyor's side of the camera or close under it, well below
    it, while every line of sight runs forward toward the pallet and steeply
    down, so nothing of the arm is in the way (tests/cell_test.cc casts the
    rays). It is also close to where the arm is after
    laying a module down, so a picture can be taken on the way back. Joint 1 is then 0.087 rad (5 degrees)
    inside its limit.
  -->
  <keyframe>
    <key name="arm_clear"
         qpos="3.141592653589793 -1.5707963267948966 1.5707963267948966 0 1.5707963267948966 0"
         ctrl="3.141592653589793 -1.5707963267948966 1.5707963267948966 0 1.5707963267948966 0"/>
  </keyframe>
</mujoco>
